+- **io_uring for socket reads; one direct send per response.** io-event picks io_uring on the
+  droplet's kernel. Reads go through the fiber scheduler, so io_uring submits the reads of every
+  connection that became ready in a reactor pass together. Batching reads and writes through the
+  ring, together with edge-triggered epoll, is what cut CPU at 40k users from about 46% to 40% on our
+  test VM. Writes have since left the ring: each response is one `write_nonblock`, which on Linux is
+  `send(2)` with `MSG_DONTWAIT` on a socket that is already non-blocking (no `fcntl`, no SQE, no
+  fiber switch), and a response of 60 B to 5 KB fits the empty send buffer essentially always. Only a
+  partial or would-block write falls back to the scheduler's io_uring write for the remainder, which
+  waits for `POLLOUT` and keeps per-connection response order. In a micro-benchmark on one pinned core
+  the ring write cost 1.1–1.5 µs per response against 0.4 µs for the direct send, even with 256
+  concurrent writers sharing each `io_uring_enter`. This fast path matters only while Ruby's
+  `RUBY_FIBER_SCHEDULER_VERSION` is 3 (Ruby 4.0.7): io-event's own optimistic direct write is compiled
+  in from version 4, so a different Ruby build should re-check `ruby/fiber/scheduler.h` before keeping it.
